all_findings.description:
The `parse_admissibility_block` function returns `list(ADMISSIBILITY_GATES)` (all five gates as failed) when `finding_text` is empty or when no `ADMISSIBILITY:` marker is found. However, it does not distinguish between "the ADMISSIBILITY block is missing entirely" and "the ADMISSIBILITY block is present but all gates failed." Both cases return the same result: all five gates in the failed list. The §17 feedback channel uses this return value to instruct models to supply missing admissibility blocks. If a model submitted a finding with all five gates legitimately FAIL, the parser would return all five gates, and the feedback channel would tell the model to "supply the missing block" — but the block isn't missing; it's present and all gates failed. The feedback would be misleading.

Evidence: Lines 55-57 and 63-64:
if not finding_text:
    return list(ADMISSIBILITY_GATES)
...
if not marker_match:
    return list(ADMISSIBILITY_GATES)
Both the empty-string case and the no-marker case return the same value as the all-gates-failed case (lines 95-112, where `failed` accumulates gates that didn't pass, and if all five fail, `failed` equals `list(ADMISSIBILITY_GATES)`).
